person('linda_k_kerber',
 'A historian of women, political ideas and citizenship in the United States. Her 1988 essay examines how a language used by historical actors became an interpretive device for historians: separate spheres can conceal changing relationships when treated as a fixed social map. In her published 2001 lecture she investigates how apparently protective exemptions and unequal obligations helped structure gendered citizenship. The entry keeps this historical analysis distinct from current legal advice and from claiming one uniform experience for all women.',
 'Women of the Republic: Intellect and Ideology in Revolutionary America (1980); “Separate Spheres, Female Worlds, Woman’s Place” (1988); No Constitutional Right to Be Ladies (1998).',
 [ev('kerber_republic','Women of the Republic',1980,[sid('kerber_iowa')],'Institutional selected bibliography confirms 1980. Complete monograph unread.'),ev('kerber_spheres','“Separate Spheres, Female Worlds, Woman’s Place”',1988,[sid('kerber_spheres')],'Journal of American History 75:1, June 1988, pp. 9–39. Selected opening/conclusion separately read.'),ev('kerber_ladies','No Constitutional Right to Be Ladies',1998,[sid('kerber_iowa')],'1998 original publication; 2001 lecture is later explanation, not the book’s publication date.')],
 [rel('women','Examines how the separate-spheres metaphor shaped both historical prescriptions and historians’ accounts of women.',[sid('kerber_spheres')],'Primary printed pp. 9–11 and 38–39 read. The critique treats the metaphor as a historically located interpretive problem, not proof that women never faced exclusions or that spaces were never gendered.'),rel('political','Investigates gendered citizenship through unequal civic burdens, exemptions and authority.',[sid('kerber_academy')],'Primary Kerber section of the Summer 2001 Bulletin talk, opening and the Reed/citizenship discussion. Robert Post’s separate introduction is not attributed to her. Historical legal examples are her account, not current-law certification.')],
 [source('kerber_spheres','Linda K. Kerber, “Separate Spheres, Female Worlds, Woman’s Place: The Rhetoric of Women’s History,” Journal of American History 75:1 (June 1988): 9–39.','https://doi.org/10.2307/1889653','Publisher citation checked at https://academic.oup.com/jah/article-abstract/75/1/9/727210 . Primary scan at https://s3.amazonaws.com/files.commons.gc.cuny.edu/wp-content/blogs.dir/2757/files/2016/06/Kerber-Separate-Spheres-Female-Worlds-Womans-Place.pdf downloaded; selected printed pp. 9–11 and 38–39 read through extracted text. Intervening essay not claimed read. The opening identifies the trope’s effect on historical choices; conclusion limits a static two-sphere model while retaining gendered power. Cited authors’ works not thereby independently read.',True),source('kerber_academy','Linda K. Kerber, “Gender and Inequality: Old Questions, New Answers,” American Academy of Arts and Sciences, Summer 2001 Bulletin, with separate introduction by Robert C. Post.','https://www.amacad.org/news/gender-and-inequality-old-questions-new-answers','Kerber’s displayed text read at the opening and Reed/civic-authority discussion. The long Post introduction is separately credited; its particular legal framework is not reassigned to Kerber. This is a published lecture account, not a full reading of No Constitutional Right to Be Ladies or independent audit of all cases. No exact delivery day inferred.',True),source('kerber_iowa','University of Iowa History, Linda K. Kerber faculty profile and bibliography.','https://history.uiowa.edu/people/linda-kerber','Institutional metadata checked for Women of the Republic 1980 and No Constitutional Right to Be Ladies 1998; original book bodies unread. Editorial collaborations are distinguished from sole authorship. The later November 2025 tenth edition listing falls outside this selected chronology; it does not imply a complete-book reading. The separately listed September 2025 joint op-ed supplies recent positive author activity.')],scope='New identity is the Iowa historian Linda K. Kerber. Primary selected essay pages and published lecture passage support two scoped contributions; monographs unread. Political/legal interpretation stays historical and source-bound. No blanket theory of women’s experience, influence inferred from acknowledgments or uncredited coauthors/editors.',
 life_sources=[life('https://history.uiowa.edu/people/linda-kerber','University of Iowa, Linda K. Kerber','Present emerita profile; selected op-eds','Positive present profile plus a jointly credited 3 September 2025 op-ed with David W. Blight. The later November 2025 edition listing is outside the selected-work chronology.'),life('https://hollisarchives.lib.harvard.edu/catalog/sch01717','Schlesinger Library, Papers of Linda K. Kerber','Biographical / historical note','Birth year 1940.','Public archival biography checked, not restricted archival contents.')],birth=1940)

person('john_lewis_gaddis',
 'A historian of the Cold War and strategy whose selected writings revisit historical explanations as evidence and perspectives change. In a 1998 primary essay he connects the opening of former communist archives with revisions to Cold War interpretation, while admitting that no historical representation can recover everything. He argues for greater attention to ideology and offers a separate, controversial account of nuclear weapons as both stabilizing and prolonging the conflict. Those are Gaddis’s interpretations, not settled judgments about comparative regimes or the whole Cold War.',
 'Strategies of Containment (1982; revised 2005 distinct); We Now Know (1997); The Landscape of History (2002).',
 [ev('gaddis_strategies','Strategies of Containment',1982,[sid('gaddis_metadata')],'British national-bibliography record gives Oxford University Press 1982. Revised 2005 version has a changed subtitle and is kept separate.'),ev('gaddis_know','We Now Know: Rethinking Cold War History',1997,[sid('gaddis_metadata')],'Clarendon 1997; the 1998 essay’s later explanation is not a publication date for the book.'),ev('gaddis_landscape','The Landscape of History',2002,[sid('gaddis_metadata')],'2002 original confirmed by national bibliography and Yale. Later paperback 2004 distinct; book body unread.')],
 [rel('political','Reassesses Cold War political power through ideology, legitimacy and newly available evidence.',[sid('gaddis_newcoldwar')],'Primary essay’s opening and second numbered argument, read. His comparative claims about regime performance and Soviet legitimacy remain attributed and disputable. This does not treat access to some archives as complete evidence.'),rel('military','Argues that nuclear deterrence could both stabilize crises and prolong the Cold War by masking other dimensions of state power.',[sid('gaddis_newcoldwar')],'Primary essay’s third argument explicitly distinguishes these two effects. It is a contested historical interpretation, not a generic military appointment arrow or a validated counterfactual for every crisis.')],
 [source('gaddis_newcoldwar','John Lewis Gaddis, “The New Cold War History,” American Diplomacy III:4 (1998), adapted from FPRI keynote, May 1998 / Footnotes, June 1998.','https://ciaotest.cc.columbia.edu/olj/ad/ad_v3_4/gaj01.html','Complete short displayed primary essay read. Locators: opening archive/representation discussion; second argument on ideology; third on nuclear weapons. Final note distinguishes May 1–2 institute address and June Footnotes publication from this journal reproduction. Claims about democracies, authoritarian states and nuclear effects remain Gaddis’s assessment; no unanimous synthesis or full-book reading asserted.',True),source('gaddis_metadata','John Lewis Gaddis, selected original/edition bibliography, Open British National Bibliography.','https://obnb.uk/a00141518-john-lewis-gaddis','Records checked for Strategies of Containment (OUP 1982), We Now Know (Clarendon 1997), Landscape (OUP 2002; later 2004), revised Strategies (2005, changed subtitle). Yale faculty bibliography independently corroborates 2002 and the new 2005 edition. Monograph bodies unread; original US/UK issue dates for other titles not inferred.')],scope='New identity is Yale Cold War historian John Lewis Gaddis, distinguished from Gaddis Smith. Primary short essay supports two specific interpretations; selected books supply publication facts only. No generic government-adviser, school-affiliation or personal influence edge. Original/revised books and address/reproduction dates remain separate.',
 life_sources=[life('https://history.yale.edu/people/john-gaddis','Yale History, John Gaddis','Present faculty biography and current writing project','Positive present teaching and ongoing hometown microhistory project.'),life('https://ead-pdfs.library.yale.edu/11232.pdf','Yale Manuscripts and Archives, John Lewis Gaddis Papers MS 2092','Biographical / historical, p. 4','Birth year 1941.','Public institutional finding aid checked; archival diaries not accessed.')],birth=1941)

person('philip_curtin',
 'A historian of Africa, forced migration and cross-cultural connections who combined quantitative reconstruction with comparative world history. Joseph C. Miller’s substantive memorial describes how the slave-trade census grew from a question about regional economic context and how its estimate prompted further archival work. The same account identifies recurring global subjects such as trade, plantations, migration and disease. The entry preserves uncertainty and the distinction between counting a forced migration and explaining enslaved people’s lived experience; it does not adopt a historical estimate as a current audited total.',
 'The Image of Africa (1964); The Atlantic Slave Trade: A Census (1969); Cross-Cultural Trade in World History (1984); On the Fringes of History: A Memoir (2005).',
 [ev('curtin_image','The Image of Africa',1964,[sid('curtin_bibliography')],'Contemporary AHA 1983 bibliography gives Wisconsin 1964. Miller’s later memorial says 1965; this discrepancy is not silently adopted.'),ev('curtin_census','The Atlantic Slave Trade: A Census',1969,['curtin',sid('curtin_bibliography')],'1969 original; estimates and subsequent revisions are not recomputed by this packet.'),ev('curtin_cross','Cross-Cultural Trade in World History',1984,[sid('curtin_cross')],'Original 1984 distinct from Cambridge online 2010 date.'),ev('curtin_memoir','On the Fringes of History: A Memoir',2005,[sid('curtin_memoir')],'Ohio University Press 2005. Memoir body unread; later digital formats not independently dated.')],
 [rel('quant','Makes source criticism and estimation central to reconstructing the scale of the Atlantic slave trade.',['curtin',sid('curtin_miller')],'Named substantive reader Joseph C. Miller’s 2009 account of the census’s origin, uncertainty and later archival responses. No fresh reading of the census or audit of modern SlaveVoyages totals; counting is not a substitute for enslaved experience.'),rel('global','Develops comparative questions around recurring cross-cultural trade, migration, plantations and disease.',[sid('curtin_miller'),sid('curtin_cross')],'Miller’s substantive account supplies the methodological characterization; the publisher record supports title/date only. A specific comparative agenda, not the claim that one historian founded all global history or a causal affiliation arrow.')],
 [source('curtin_miller','Joseph C. Miller, “Philip D. Curtin (1922–2009),” AHA Perspectives, 1 November 2009.','https://www.historians.org/perspectives-article/philip-d-curtin-1922-2009/','Memorial’s sections on comparative research and the census’s origin/method/reception read, plus life opening. Argument characterization is explicitly mediated by Miller. His contemporary assessment of estimates is not adopted as a current data audit; his organizational-founding dates and polemical defence of Curtin are not used. Image of Africa is dated 1965 here but 1964 in Curtin’s contemporary AHA bibliography; latter retained.',True),source('curtin_cross','Philip D. Curtin, Cross-Cultural Trade in World History (Cambridge University Press, 1984).','https://www.cambridge.org/core/books/cross-cultural-trade-in-world-history/0FD4DBE86A2B1593ACC355F9D28D8C15','Indexed publisher title/author and original print date 25 May 1984 checked, distinct from online 15 March 2010. Direct open timed out. Description and contents are not treated as reading of the chapters; substantive methodological claims use Miller.'),source('curtin_bibliography','Philip D. Curtin, bibliography in AHA 1983 presidential booklet.','https://www.historians.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/AM_1983_Presidential-Booklet.pdf','Contemporary author bibliography checked: Image of Africa (Wisconsin 1964), Census (Wisconsin 1969). Author/coauthor/editor headings kept distinct. Source supports bibliographic facts only; scanned OCR of other titles not used uncritically.'),source('curtin_memoir','Philip D. Curtin, On the Fringes of History: A Memoir (Ohio University Press, 2005), BiblioVault record.','https://www.bibliovault.org/BV.book.epl?ISBN=9780821416457','Publisher-distributor record checked for 2005 title and imprint. Duplicated author string on page is one author repeated, not coauthorship. Body unread.')],old=['curtin'],scope='Existing philip_curtin profile reused. New arguments use named substantive reader Miller; the books remain unread and numerical estimates are not freshly certified. The earlier Hodgson/editor-preface bibliography remains limited to its own scope and supplies no new influence claim. The 1964/1965 discrepancy is explicit, and the contemporary bibliography supports 1964.',
 life_sources=[life('https://www.historians.org/perspectives-article/philip-d-curtin-1922-2009/','Joseph C. Miller, Philip D. Curtin (1922–2009), AHA','Opening biographical paragraphs','Born 1922; died 4 June 2009 at West Chester.','Contemporary scholarly memorial life facts checked.')],birth=1922,death=2009,death_date='2009-06-04',life_limit='Identity matched through the same titles and career; later reprints are not life evidence.')

person('francois_furet',
 'A historian of the French Revolution and political ideas whose work challenged an established social interpretation of revolution. In the opening of his 1971 essay he attacks what he calls a Jacobin historiography, distinguishing its commemorative commitments from critical inquiry. Gérard Grunberg’s substantive 1979 reading identifies a further argument about political language and the representation of popular sovereignty. Both the polemic and its later interpretation remain attributed; the entry does not endorse either a general verdict on Marx or an inevitable path from 1789 to twentieth-century regimes.',
 '“Le Catéchisme révolutionnaire” (1971); Penser la Révolution française (1978, including revised earlier pieces); Interpreting the French Revolution, translated by Elborg Forster (1981); Le Passé d’une illusion (1995).',
 [ev('furet_catechisme','“Le Catéchisme révolutionnaire”',1971,[sid('furet_catechisme')],'Annales 26:2, pp. 255–289. Original article distinguished from revised inclusion in 1978.'),ev('furet_penser','Penser la Révolution française',1978,[sid('furet_penser')],'Gallimard 1978; earlier component essays retain 1971 dates.'),ev('furet_interpreting','Interpreting the French Revolution: Elborg Forster translation',1981,[sid('furet_translation')],'English Cambridge/MSH 1981, separately dated and translator credited.',kind='translation'),ev('furet_illusion','Le Passé d’une illusion',1995,[sid('furet_academie')],'French work dated 1995 in institutional biography; later English translation not assigned this original date. Book unread.')],
 [rel('marx','Challenges a specific Marxist/Jacobin historiography of the French Revolution for reproducing revolutionary and commemorative categories.',[sid('furet_catechisme')],'Primary pp. 255–256 read. Furet’s polemical label and diagnosis remain his claims about a specific historiographical dispute, not a critique of every Marxist or a personal arrow to Marx.' ,'critique'),rel('political','Examines political language and claims to represent the people as mechanisms of revolutionary power.',[sid('furet_grunberg')],'Named reader Gérard Grunberg, pp. 895 and 897, on Penser. Full book unread; no universal acceptance, deterministic twentieth-century analogy or inference from institutional membership.')],
 [source('furet_catechisme','François Furet, “Le Catéchisme révolutionnaire,” Annales E.S.C. 26:2 (1971): 255–289.','https://www.persee.fr/doc/ahess_0395-2649_1971_num_26_2_422355','Primary opening pp. 255–256 read through Persée extraction; p. 257 retrieval failed and remaining article unread. The polemic answers criticism of Furet/Richet’s joint work and labels a particular dominant historiography Marxist/Jacobin. The revised 1978 collection text is not assumed identical to this original. Diagnosis and political judgments stay attributed.',True),source('furet_grunberg','Gérard Grunberg, review of Furet, Penser la Révolution française, Revue française de science politique 29:4–5 (1979): 895–898.','https://www.persee.fr/doc/rfsp_0035-2950_1979_num_29_4_418652_t1_0895_0000_000','Selected printed pp. 895 and 897 read, including the relationship among old/new essays and the interpretation of political language and popular sovereignty. Pp. 896 and 898 not claimed read. This named substantive reading is not Évelyne Ritaine’s different review; full Furet book unread.',True),source('furet_penser','François Furet, Penser la Révolution française (Paris: Gallimard, 1978), BnF original record.','https://catalogue.bnf.fr/ark:/12148/cb34608906f','Author, original Gallimard 1978, 259-page record checked. Later Folio page counts do not replace original pagination. Book body unread.'),source('furet_translation','François Furet, Interpreting the French Revolution, translated by Elborg Forster (Cambridge University Press / Éditions de la Maison des Sciences de l’Homme, 1981).','https://search.worldcat.org/fr/title/461652610','Institutional bibliographic metadata and contemporary AHR review metadata https://academic.oup.com/ahr/article-abstract/87/4/1103/90379 checked for 1981 co-imprint and Forster translation. English body not read or collated with the French.'),source('furet_academie','Académie française, François Furet biography and selected works.','https://www.academie-francaise.fr/les-immortels/francois-furet?election=20-03-1997&fauteuil=1','Institutional biography records Le Passé d’une illusion 1995, birth 1927 and death 12 July 1997. Bibliographic/life fields only; celebratory evaluation not adopted, and no reading of the communism book or translation claimed.')],scope='New identity matched to the French-Revolution historian and BnF/Académie records. Argument support is the bounded original essay and Grunberg’s expressly named reading. Furet/Richet coauthorship of earlier work remains distinct; no group work is reassigned. Original essay, revised collection, translation and later monograph stay separate. His criticism targets a particular historical interpretation, not a personal causal influence or blanket theory classification.',
 life_sources=[life('https://catalogue.bnf.fr/ark:/12148/cb11903742f.public','BnF authority, Furet, François (1927–1997)','Naissance / Mort','Born 1927; died 12 July 1997 at Toulouse; authority cites civil death record.','National-library life fields checked.'),life('https://www.academie-francaise.fr/les-immortels/francois-furet?election=20-03-1997&fauteuil=1','Académie française, François Furet','Biographical closing','Confirms death 12 July 1997 before formal reception.')],birth=1927,death=1997,death_date='1997-07-12',life_limit='Death day supported by both BnF and Académie; election, reception and publication dates are not confused with death.')

def write(path,value):
 p=HERE/path;p.parent.mkdir(parents=True,exist_ok=True);p.write_text(json.dumps(value,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
N={n['id']:n for n in G['nodes']}; P={p['id']:p for p in G['people']}; S={s['id']:s for s in G['sources']}
assert len(DATA)==10
notes=[]; node_additions=[]
for d in DATA:
 pid=d['pid']; q=next(x for x in Q['persons'] if x['person_id']==pid);label=q['name']
